      "text": "Since $y = \\frac{1}{2} x$ and $x = \\frac{1}{5} y$, we can write this as: $$\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{1}{2} x \\\\ x &= \\frac{1}{5} y \\end{aligned}$$\nMultiplying the second equation by 2, then multiplying both sides by 5, we end up with: $$\\begin{aligned}5x &= \\frac{1}{5} y \\cdot 2 \\\\ % above string moved in from 2^b to be more aligned 5 x &= \\frac{2}{5} y \\\\ % previous end-up line transformed here 5x &= \\boxed{\\text{x is 12}}% above string moved in from 2^b to be more aligned y x = \\text{(grab $y$ to get $\\frac{2}{5}$ of it)}} \\end{aligned}$$\nSetting $y$ equal to $\\frac{1}{5} y (12)$ and solving, we find that $y = 24$. Then from part (1) we find that $x = 12$. The answer is thus $x = 12$.",
